A||||9||Patrick||Sometimes it's the simplest things...||||3||
Z||000000||Patrick||04-20-2000||02:56 PM||||<BR>I noticed over the last week or so that my engine was turning over slower and slower every time I started it. The usual problems were going through my head - Was it the battery? Was it the charging system? Was it the starter?<P>Yesterday it wouldn't turn over at all. I tried to jump start it. No go, still wouldn't turn over. This gave me one more item to worry about... the jumper cables.<P>Next step was to remove the battery and try the one from my Chev. Only problem is that this battery is 10 1/8 inches long. It took some doing to shoehorn it in there. The end of the battery was awful close to the pulley guard of the serpentine belt on the 4-banger. As I was wrestling the cables around trying to attach them to the battery, it became VERY obvious what the problem had been all along. The end of the cable that goes to the starter slid right out of the clamp that attaches to the positive battery terminal. It probably had been loose for some time and my screwing around pulled it out completely. Man, did I feel stupid. (But also glad that I found the problem <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>Lesson for today - Check the simple things first!||||reg||3
Z||000001||TheFiero64||04-21-2000||06:26 PM||||At lease it was a lot cheaper than a battery or starter. you were lucky  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000002||Patrick||04-22-2000||03:32 AM||||<BR>Well, maybe I spoke too soon... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>I began to have problems again. Sometimes the Fiero would start fine, and at other times absolutely nothing would happen when I turned the ignition key. Luckily I could push-start the car in a pinch, but this was rather awkward to say the least.<P>I discovered that one of the two long thin bolts that run the length of the starter and holds it together had gotten loose and fallen right out. Although the starter stayed in one piece, it was a bit **** -eyed. This probably explained why the starter was turning over slower than normal. It also resulted in the copper strap that connects the starter to the solenoid to crack in two. Sometimes the two halves would still make contact with each other and the car would start. At other times the two halves would not be touching and therefore no power would get to the starter.<P>Getting a replacement bolt shouldn't be a problem, but I'll have to improvise a bit to fix the copper strap. It's part of the field assembly of the starter, and if I can't fix the strap, I might have to buy a rebuilt starter. (But that's definitely a <I>last</I> resort. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>Today's lesson - Even after finding what appears to be the problem, never assume that nothing else needs to be checked.<p>[This message has been edited by Patrick (edited 04-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick||04-22-2000||03:40 AM||||<BR>Geez this software censors the tamest words... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The word in my previous post that was "cleaned up" is <I>c_o_c_k-e_y_e_d</I>.||||reg||1
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||04-22-2000||06:07 AM||||<B>POTTY MOUTH</B>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Careful with those starters they're heavy. I was taking mine off and it slipped out of my hands and fell on my chest. I think it broke a rib. I was gonna go to the doctor, but all he would do is take my money and say "You have a broken rib." I have broken 4 ribs before... they don't hurt too much.||||reg||7
Z||000005||Patrick||04-24-2000||01:57 AM||||<BR>Hey Chris, I know what it's like. I cracked a rib while playing hockey. Geez, did it ever hurt when I sneezed! Thank gawd it wasn't allergy season. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Ok, back to the task at hand...<P>I was able to repair the starter after all, works like a charm. Since I can reliably start the engine again (when the engine is cold), I can now carry on with my research into the <I>hot start</I> problem. There's always something... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||04-24-2000||06:19 AM||||Why don't you replace the starter? If its getting sensitive to heat why go through the process of finding the heat resistance? Are you that glutton for punishment? EEK! <P>I did the same with an alternator. I tried to rebuild it and when it would heat up it would die out. I was trying to rebuild it to save money. Buying a new one got me on the road without having to worry any more about the next problem and it has a lifetime warranty! Spend less time fixing and more time driving!||||reg||10
Z||000007||turbo86se||04-24-2000||12:18 PM||||I had the same problem with my 86SE when I first bought it. One day it simply would not start!!  After previously replacing the neutral saftey switch three times and a installing a new battery, I was a little "over" the car not starting. I towed my beloved baby to my mechanic, and he said before he was even going to look at the starter, he was going to brush off the battery terminal connections and see what that does. Well, it fired right up! ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Racer 1||04-24-2000||01:58 PM||||I got a older ford starter relay mounted in my engine bay. You can get them from JcWhitney in a kit. My 2.5 duck would not turn over when the engine was hot I kept replacing Batties and starters finally I saw the hot weather starter relay in JcWhitney bamb have not had any problems with the starter in 3 years in 100+ degree weather outside. THe starter siliniod heats up and doesnt start good on the stock OEM setup.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Patrick||04-24-2000||04:14 PM||||<BR>Sorry guys, I guess I should've mentioned that the <I>hot start</I> problems have nothing to do with the starter. The engine turns over fine when it's hot, it just won't start.<P>Some of us have been discussing the <I>hot start</I> problem here:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/002700.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/002700.html</A> ||||reg||1
